def build(course, m):
    return course(96, 'Coastal Livelihoods Planning Practicum',
        'Coastal Livelihoods\nPlanning Practicum',
        'Prepare a reviewable coastal livelihood plan with a bounded choice, accountable delivery and evidence of benefits and risks.', [
        m('Build an options brief for a coastal site',
            'Compare two feasible options against shared criteria and an evidence baseline.',
            """
The fictional Oru Reach team has a disrupted fish landing and an expensive cold-room proposal. Define the immediate service problem before choosing a project. Some workers need dependable unloading and an affordable way to preserve stock. Others report that warnings arrive after access paths close. Record these as distinct issues and examine the links between them. [S81] [S82]

Set out the status quo, a staffed alternate route and a larger facility proposal. Compare all three across livelihood access, cost, continuity, marine impacts and who can decide. The same criterion should be applied using comparable observations. A new facility's planned capacity and an existing landing's observed use should carry different evidence labels. [S84]

Use an original role map for boat crew, processors, households, markets, association leaders, social services and authorities. Add the people absent from official records and explain how you would invite input in a real authorised process. Findings about coastal participation in Lagos reveal questions about timing and accessibility. The supplied fiction defines the group and constraints for this exercise. [R92]

Describe an evidence baseline and an unresolved claim before making a recommendation. For example, record how many people can reach the market during the tested opening window, and whether the alternative would transfer costs. Label a group count by period and eligibility. Note who can confirm the physical or rights questions, and give a decision milestone tied to the evidence. [S83] Describe resources needed for each option and how it will operate in the same rough season. Include a clear uncertainty that could reverse the recommendation.
            """,
            'Oru Reach has repeat landing closures. A sponsor proposes a cold room, but affordable access, reliable power and an alternative unloading route are unresolved.',
            'Compare the current route and two options. Include access, work, safety dependencies, marine impacts, operating costs and review authority.',
            'Show evidence and assumptions for each option. Identify a feasible small test and the decisions needing formal review.',
            """
Start from the service need.|What problem should the brief describe first?|Workers lack dependable unloading and preservation access.|The sponsor needs a bigger project name.|All workers need the same intervention.|A funding offer is automatically the problem definition.|A proposal should answer an evidenced problem.
Compare plausible alternatives.|What makes the options appraisal useful?|Use shared criteria and comparable observations across feasible routes.|Present planned data as observed only for the preferred route.|Omit current access from every comparison.|Compare the sponsor's reputation against unrelated worker concerns.|Common criteria help a reviewer judge the trade-offs.
Map affected groups.|Who may need an explicit voice in a plan drafted from boat owner reports?|Processors, crew and households relying on the catch.|Only the promoter's board.|Only people already served by the facility.|Only the author of the options memo.|Direct effects can extend beyond the group listed in a register.
Label evidence status.|What should planned cold-room capacity be called at this stage?|A design assumption or specification requiring verification.|Observed service use.|Guaranteed household access.|Proof of better incomes.|A specification describes what the design anticipates.
Clarify decision authority.|How should the brief treat an unresolved real shore access right?|Identify evidence and refer it to the competent decision route.|Declare the right settled from classroom work.|Give the sponsor sole control by default.|Delete it from the brief.|Authority and evidence are both necessary for a real decision.
            """),
        m('Rehearse safeguards and contingencies',
            'Test participation, interruption response and proposed community benefits before a simulated decision.',
            """
Turn the important assumptions into simple test cards. One card can check whether a worker without an owner registration obtains an appointment. Another can check whether a missed warning has a usable fallback. A third can test whether a seasonal route appears correctly in a draft proposal. Specify the starting condition, response, role and evidence of completion. [S81] [S82]

In a representative exercise, enter concerns through two channels and assign each to someone with a response deadline. Ask the learner to explain the process to a person who speaks another language or cannot attend a public meeting. Capture what the person is able to influence and how they would learn the decision. Record changes and reasons clearly. [S83]

A benefit test can trace a worker from eligibility to actual usable receipt. If a trial offers six morning slots and late arrivals miss them, review why. Slot capacity alone may hide constraints from transport or the timing of catches. Consider who bears new costs and how the project can use the limited data proportionately. [S84]

Use a written record with the date, scenario, observation, status and next responsible action. Raise failures that are consequential even if another part of the rehearsal passes. A blocked route can require immediate reconsideration of the exercise proposal. A suspected technical danger or real conflict belongs with the appropriate qualified or authorised team. Revise a failed step and rerun the same case. [S82] Check who is responsible for reopening a failed step. The review should distinguish steps the team can revise from questions that need an external decision.
            """,
            'The rehearsal succeeds for registered owners. A processor misses the warning, and another worker cannot use the facility during the available hours.',
            'Write three exception cards and assign an owner, expected result and retest for each.',
            'Test actual access for workers outside the easiest route. Track the failed cases and retest the corrected steps.',
            """
Define reproducible test cards.|What goes into an interruption card?|Initial condition, expected action, responsible role and observed result.|Only an assertion that the service works.|A future date without a scenario.|The author's preference alone.|A repeatable test can assess a defined response.
Include people outside the register.|Which case most directly tests a registration barrier?|A processor without an owner record requests the proposed service.|A registered owner follows the usual route.|The sponsor downloads a publicity page.|The organiser prints additional invitations.|An exception case tests who a standard route might omit.
Follow up on concerns.|What demonstrates that a submission received responsible attention?|A named role records the response and tells the contributor what happens next.|A page view is recorded.|The meeting is closed before response.|The concern is discarded after typing.|Accountability includes feedback and action.
Measure benefit at the last stage.|Which evidence shows that a worker could use a benefit in practice?|An eligible worker received and used a suitable available slot.|The facility advertised six slots.|A person clicked the offer once.|The plan includes projected bookings.|Effective benefit requires a usable service.
Retest a failed assumption.|After correcting a missed warning route, what should the team do?|Rerun the relevant scenario and record whether the person can act.|Count the wording revision as success.|Replace the case with an easy example.|Omit the finding from the record.|Retesting connects changes to the original failure.
            """),
        m('Present a decision and monitoring plan',
            'Deliver an accountable decision memo with measures, ownership and a review date.',
            """
Write the immediate decision first. A classroom decision might proceed with a staffed rehearsal, revise a proposal or pause pending a formal assessment. State the main evidence and the need it addresses. If the known barrier is physical access at landing time, a cold room alone may leave the practical problem unresolved. [S81]

Show baseline, measure, time window, outcome and one important limit. Use a denominator that names the group. Record both people who received a service and people excluded before application. Keep outcomes distinct from meetings, offers or completed works. Include a way to track impacts on nearby areas, especially where development or adaptation could transfer burdens. [S82] [S84]

Decide when a review should change course. For example, two failed exception tests can require a redesign before another round. Identify which role can pause, what evidence would permit a retest and how participants keep access to essential routes. Distinguish a decision to test a fictional design from approval for a real activity, expenditure or restriction. [S83]

Write the final brief so someone who did not attend the exercise can evaluate it. Include a stated need, options considered, affected groups and environment, supporting and adverse findings, decision, responsible roles and review date. Explain one thing that would change your recommendation and who would verify it. Record outstanding concerns in a confidential or aggregate form appropriate to the audience. [S84] State how the team would compare the next trial with the one just completed. Use the same inclusion rules and record any design or staffing changes that affect interpretation.
            """,
            'Eight of ten ordinary cases used the route, but none of the tested low-cost users could book a suitable time. The sponsor proposes expansion.',
            'Draft a decision memo with the observed results, an access improvement, a stop condition and a dated recheck.',
            'Retain the successful ordinary cases and the failed low-cost cases. Link any next trial to a verified change in access.',
            """
State a decision and its grounds.|Which first sentence fits this evidence?|Revise booking before expansion because the tested low-cost route failed.|Expand because some ordinary cases passed.|Declare the whole village served.|Count the proposal as completed work.|The decision should reflect the practical failure as well as the successes.
Measure comparable access.|What does eight of ten describe?|The proportion using the route among ordinary cases in the exercise.|The percentage of all coastal residents protected.|An effect on fish stocks.|A national enrolment rate.|The group and scope define the measure.
Monitor adverse findings.|Which observation should stay visible in the memo?|No tested low-cost user could book a usable slot.|Only that the proposal has a title.|Only support from the sponsor.|Only the average with failures omitted.|Unequal access can be obscured by a combined total.
Define when to review.|Which recheck condition supports a defensible next step?|A named role verifies a revised low-cost slot route against the failed cases by an agreed date.|Restart after printing the policy.|Assume more demand will solve the barrier.|Close the concern if time passes.|Review needs ownership, a clear observation and timing.
Brief an independent reader.|What should a reader be able to trace?|How the evidence and affected groups led to a justified, conditional decision.|Only the names of the report writers.|Only the headlines without underlying facts.|Only the proposed marketing benefits.|Traceability lets another reader examine the decision.
            """),
    ])
